{"parentUuid":"408942dc-fdcc-4375-821d-02a1bcf31c84","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"57243a42-7471-47a0-8091-068cfa38ca55","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DSjBvuwdo8UJg3r5c9LtMs","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all needed evidence. Here is the punch list.\n\n---\n\n## Efficiency Findings — `migrate.go`\n\n### 1. `writeMigratedFinalFullCurrent` flattens the entire `/full/current` tree\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:478-510`\n**Issue:** Calls `FlattenTree` over the whole root tree (every shard/checkpoint/session entry across all previously migrated checkpoints), then `BuildTreeFromEntries` rebuilds the whole thing. For a partial final batch on a `/full/current` that already contains many checkpoints, this is O(all entries) per call. The production path (`updateCommittedFullTranscript` → `flattenCheckpointEntries` + `spliceCheckpointSubtree`/`UpdateSubtree`) uses O(depth) tree surgery scoped to the touched shard.\n**Suggested fix:** For each checkpoint in `pendingFull`, call `flattenCheckpointEntries(rootTreeHash, cp.checkpointID.Path())` and `spliceCheckpointSubtree(...)` (looped, threading the new root tree hash forward) instead of flatten-the-world + rebuild.\n**Severity:** nice-to-have. The final partial batch is bounded (≤100 by default) and only runs once per migration, but it walks all *previously migrated* checkpoints written to /full/current — which after a re-migration into existing data can be substantial.\n\n### 2. O(N×M) raw-artifact pruning in `writeMigratedFinalFullCurrent`\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:493-499`\n**Issue:** `for each rawArtifactPaths { for each entries { matches() } }`. With ≤100 sessions in the final batch and the existing `entries` already being walked in (1), this is N (≤100) × M (all entries on /full/current) string comparisons per call. M dominates and (1) already walks everything.\n**Suggested fix:** Subsumed by (1). If (1) is not adopted, build a lookup keyed by session-path prefix instead and probe entries by membership rather than scanning.\n**Severity:** nice-to-have (only matters if (1) stays as-is).\n\n### 3. Slice round-trip via `migratedFullEntrySet.toMap()` in archived-generation path\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:551`, `631-643`, `653-655`\n**Issue:** `buildMigratedFullEntrySet` appends entries into slices, then `writeMigratedFullGeneration` immediately calls `.toMap()` to convert back to a `map[string]TreeEntry` for `BuildTreeFromEntries`. That means the archived-generation path (the common case in big migrations) allocates a slice, then a map, copying every entry. Previously the function wrote directly into a single map.\n**Suggested fix:** Keep a map-shaped result for the archived path. Either (a) have `buildMigratedFullEntrySet` accept and write into a `map[string]TreeEntry` plus a separate small slice for `rawArtifactPaths`, or (b) only build slices when `writeMigratedFinalFullCurrent` actually needs the prune-list metadata.\n**Severity:** nice-to-have. With ~100 checkpoints per generation each holding chunked transcript blobs (often few entries per session), the extra allocations are modest but real, and they happen on every archived batch.\n\n### 4. `pendingFull` reallocated on each flush\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:274` (`pendingFull = make([]migratedFullCheckpoint, 0, batchSize)`)\n**Issue:** Reallocating is the right call here — `writeMigratedFullGeneration` retains the previous slice during its work, and `pendingFull[:0]` would keep the backing array (and its referenced `[]byte` transcripts) alive until overwritten. The current code is correct.\n**Suggested fix:** None.\n**Severity:** opinion — current code is fine; do not change to `[:0]`.\n\n### 5. `RotateCurrentGenerationIfNeeded` re-walks the tree we just wrote\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:282`\n**Issue:** Right after `writeMigratedFinalFullCurrent` builds and commits a new tree, `RotateCurrentGenerationIfNeeded` calls `GetRefState` + `CountCheckpointsInTree` on it (v2_generation.go:481-491). The migration code already knows the post-merge entry set and could compute the count without a re-walk.\n**Suggested fix:** Don't change it. The rotation path is shared with production and the safety re-read is intentional (concurrency guard). Cost is one tree walk on the just-written tree, dwarfed by the migration as a whole.\n**Severity:** opinion — leave as-is.\n\n### 6. Test parallelism vs `migrateMaxCheckpointsPerGeneration` global\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate_test.go:193-196, 245-248, 305-308, 426-429, 629-632`\n**Issue:** Five tests mutate the package-global `migrateMaxCheckpointsPerGeneration` and therefore omit `t.Parallel()`, while ~15 sibling tests in the same file do parallelize. This is a quality concern, not hot-path efficiency, but the fix is one line: make `migrateCheckpointsV2` accept a `batchSize int` (or wrap state in a small `migrateOptions` struct) and pass it through `migrateFullBatchSize()`. Tests then pass values directly and stay parallel.\n**Suggested fix:** Thread `batchSize` as a parameter instead of relying on the package var; restore `t.Parallel()` in the five tests.\n**Severity:** nice-to-have.\n\n### 7. Recurring `repo.TreeObject(taskTreeHash)` if duplicates appear\n**Where:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:701-713`\n**Issue:** `cp.taskTrees[session.sessionIndex]` is read once per session; if the same `taskTreeHash` ever appeared more than once across sessions/checkpoints in one batch we'd reload+flatten it repeatedly. Looking at how `taskTrees` is populated (one entry per task tool-use ID per session), duplicates are unlikely in practice.\n**Severity:** opinion — skip.\n\n### Skipped (cost negligible)\n- `migratedRawArtifactPaths.matches` three string ops per call.\n- `nextGeneration == 0` lazy resolve — one-time.\n- `entries.rawEntries` slice growth — go-git tree-build cost dominates.\n- Idempotence/no-op-commit on re-migration with ide